WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
In the European Union we succeed with so many things 
and it's 2023 and we still haven’t succeeded with 

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.320
the thing which should be obvious and natural: 
gender equality. We are pushing hard for the 

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:20.340
next EU health strategy to recognise sexual and &nbsp;
reproductive health rights, that this standard will

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:26.640
be a common standard, from the perspective 
of health rights, for all EU citizens.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.240
 

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.380
Mr Biedrón, thanks for being with us. It's already
evening in Brussels. You're just arriving from the

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.480
airport. You’re coming from Poland, your home
country. Well, Poland is a country that has been

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.300
in the spotlight in the last few years 
for the deterioration of women's rights.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.120
This Parliament has been very vocal about it. 
It has criticised the de facto ban on abortion, 

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.940
it has asked the European Union to do something 
to change the situation. How far can 

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:12.420
the European Union go in that sense?
Thank you for having me. Of course, we see 

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.700
the deterioration of women's rights in Poland. 
In today's Poland women have less

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.600
rights than when Poland was entering the European Union 
in 2004. So you see there is something going wrong.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.900
And this backlash is not seen only in Poland but 
in other member states. What the European Union should do 

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:39.240
is to create finally a union of equal rights 
for everyone. It shouldn't be a dream, since 

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:49.080
we created a standardisation of the roaming
system, of charges. Why should we not create a system 

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:56.700
of standard rights for women’s rights? 
This is not the case in 2023 in the European Union, 

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:03.300
which should lead by example.
But when we talk about reproductive rights and abortion 

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:10.740
that falls under national competencies, right?
Some critics say that it would be meddling 

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:16.240
in national affairs to try to change something in that regard. 
What do you respond to that?

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:23.340
Women's rights are human rights. From this perspective, 
should a woman have a choice to decide 

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.580
about her body or her life? 
It's her body, her life, and it should be her choice. 

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:38.520
It is not the case in many member states.
When we see the situation where in today's Poland, 

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:45.900
because of the judgment of the so-called 
constitutional tribunal - which says de facto that 

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:54.180
women cannot execute a legal and safe abortion - 
because of that, six citizens died, 

00:02:54.180 --> 00:03:02.220
there is something wrong. We need to improve it. 
When we see that among seven million girls and women 

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:10.440
who passed the EU border with Ukraine, 
the Ukrainian refugees, there are girls raped by 

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:17.280
Russian soldiers, and they’re coming to Poland and 
they're finding out that in Poland they cannot 

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:23.580
terminate this rape. There's something wrong with
the European Union for not giving these rights.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.800
Which tools does the European Union have 
to pressure national governments to change that?

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:38.460
We have a charter of fundamental rights, we have
treaties that say that everyone is equal, that 

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:45.420
we should have the same standards concerning human rights. 
As a committee dealing with women's rights 

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:52.740
and gender equality, we are pushing hard for
the next EU health strategy to recognise sexual 

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:58.320
and reproductive health rights, that this standard 
will be a common standard, from the perspective 

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:05.640
of health rights, for all EU citizens.
Another important issue that you're dealing with 

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.820
this year is violence against women. The European 
Commission has put on the table a proposal to create 

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:18.180
common rules for all EU countries to deal with it.
Why is it important to have common rules on that?

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:25.080
We are tackling so many crimes, and violence 
against women is not recognised by the European Union 

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.500
as a euro crime. It should be a standard.
There are two important tools which we can use.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.380
The first one is mentioned by you: a special 
directive combating violence against women and 

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:45.540
domestic violence. We started to work on that. 
The second important file, which helps us to

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:51.060
have standards for combating violence against girls and 
women, is of course the Istanbul Convention. 

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.280
Just to explain quickly to people who are watching us: 
the Istanbul Convention is the main pan-European

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:02.760
instrument to fight violence against girls and women.
Which should be ratified by all member states.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.760
It’s not the case. And it should be ratified by
the European Union to make one standard for all, 

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.620
for example, concerning data collection. 
I know that Parliament had been asking for the European 

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:20.820
Union to be a party in the convention as well...
The Council has just proposed to the

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:28.800
Parliament that it will give the green light to the 
ratification of the Istanbul Convention. It is a very much

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:36.180
awaited sign, especially in the context of the 
war in Ukraine, where we have millions of girls 

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:43.560
and women fleeing from Ukraine, seeking protection 
and finding out that there is no one standard

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.560
within the European Union concerning the protection of 
girls and women against violence.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:56.280
In practice, what would change by the European Union,
as a whole body, ratifying this convention?

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:02.000
Would this change the fact that six European countries haven’t
ratified the convention yet?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:12.060
It will bring standardisation concerning protection, 
prevention... I mentioned data collection, but it will also have

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:18.180
an impact on the education of girls and women, boys and men.
It will bring standardisation in the help for the victims 

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:23.520
of violence. All the tools which will bring
the one approach of the European Union, the common 

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.240
approach towards fighting violence 
against girls and women. It will be complementary

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:37.500
 to the proposed directive by the European Commission. 
Cyber violence against girls and women 

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:43.860
also will be addressed by this directive proposal 
by the European Commission. The Istanbul convention is not 

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:50.640
mentioning it because it's an older document and
this phenomenon is developing on the Internet.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.360
We, as European Parliament, will make sure that 
both of these documents will work together.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.540
And be complementary. When it comes to gender equality
in the workplace, there have been some positive

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.520
developments in the European Union in terms of 
legislation. This Parliament has just recently&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:17.220
approved new rules that introduce quotas for women
in the top jobs of big companies. Why was it

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:22.680
necessary? Why is it necessary to,&nbsp;
in 2023, introduce quotas for women?

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:29.100
I know. It is surprising to me as well why it's necessary, 
but it’s really needed. These kind of tools are helping 

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:38.220
women to achieve what for many men, and I say it as
a male politician, we are receiving as that,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:44.820
just because we are men. In the European Union only a little 
bit more than eight percent of CEOs of

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:51.120
of the companies are women. Eight percent! 
And women are more than half of society. 

00:07:51.120 --> 00:08:01.140
There is a glass ceiling which stops women achieving 
these high positions in companies. Women are more 

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:08.220
educated. 61% of university graduates 
in the European Union are women. So what happens

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:15.900
that they are not getting these high profile jobs?
It is, of course, this patriarchal system.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:26.280
This directive of women on boards is trying to deal with it
by creating the opportunity for women to be represented 

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:36.240
in publicly listed companies. I can tell you that 
I used to be a mayor. And when I became

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:43.200
a mayor I started to manage municipal companies.
In these municipal companies there were only men, 

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.620
to my surprise. I asked these men: “Why are there 
no women?” And they said: “Women are not interested 

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:56.760
in environment cases, in water supply, in cleaning
the streets and so on and so on.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:03.540
Which is not true, of course. Then I introduced &nbsp;
quotas and suddenly it appeared that there were many 

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:10.440
women who wanted to be in these well-paid jobs
jobs and take responsibility for water supply,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:18.960
for environment issues in the city, and so on and so on.
So just give women the chance and they are very well 

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.840
prepared to take over.
Another new rule that this Parliament is getting ready

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:30.120
to vote on is the one that we call &nbsp;
 the paid transparency, which will require 

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:36.000
big companies to disclose the salaries 
that they pay to men and women. I understand 

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:43.320
that this is intended to reduce the pay gap, 
the gender pay gap. Can you explain how this

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:50.460
legislation will be able to do this?
The gender pay gap is a reality. Our mothers, 

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:58.680
our grandmothers, our sisters, our female co-workers,
they earn less. Their pensions are smaller than 

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:05.760
men’s pensions. This is a reality. The gender pay gap 
in European Union is 13%. In some member states 

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:14.520
it’s more, in some member states it's less. But it's 
a reality and we need to approach this issue. 

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:23.220
For us the goal was clear. The more transparency we 
have in this field, the more visible inequalities 

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:32.460
are and it's easier to approach that. I think
it's is a fair treatment of the workers 

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:41.880
that they should know that, while you have the same
level of knowledge and your effort is the same 

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:48.180
and your engagement in the work place is the same, 
you should be paid equally. And this is not the case.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:54.540
To conclude,  I’d like to ask you a rather provocative question. 
You are a man. You were chairing a committee

00:10:54.540 --> 00:11:02.220
dedicated to women's rights. Isn't it a paradox?
I bet you've heard this question many times already.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:10.800
Yes, I have heard it a lot. And I must say that I’m proud&nbsp;
to be chairing the women's rights and gender equality 

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.520
committee, because I believe that gender equality
is for men and women. It's not only for women.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:25.500
We, as men, should be in solidary with girls and women,
we should be supporting girls and women. 

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:34.640
There is a special place in hell for men not supporting&nbsp;women.
Do we need more education for men on gender equality?

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.980
Of course. And it should start with boys, 
not only men. It should start from the

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:48.480
early school, with understanding inequalities.
I think we would be all more sensitive if this 

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:58.500
was a standard thing. In the European Union we succeed
with so many things and it's 2023 and we still 

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:04.680
haven’t succeeded with the thing which should be
obvious and natural: gender equality. We still 

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:12.060
treat women like they don't have equal rights. 
All statistics show that we still need 60 years

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:20.940
to achieve gender equality in the European Union. 
It's a lot. We would think it’s a paradise, 

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.000
but for many girls and women it's still a hell, 
and it shouldn't be.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.680
Thank you very much. 

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:31.320
From the European Parliament, I'm Márcia Bizzotto and this is
Robert Biedrón, chair of the Parliament's

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:36.480
committee on gender equality and women's rights. 
Thank you very much. It was my honour.

